{"term":"badyl","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Slang for penis, literally a 'plant stalk' or 'weed stem'; shape metaphor that can carry a diminutive or mocking connotation about thickness.","english_term":"stalk","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Agricultural/botanical primary meaning (tall weed stalk) gives a rustic flavor; the thinness of a plant stalk can imply a less impressive size; mildly humorous rather than aggressively crude."}
{"term":"tyczka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":45,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Slang for penis, literally a 'small pole' or 'stake'; shape metaphor often implying a thin or narrow penis.","english_term":"pole","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Also the standard term in 'skok o tyczce' (pole vault); the '-ka' diminutive can imply thinness rather than impressive girth; the athletic double meaning is widely recognized and adds a humorous dimension."}
{"term":"siusiak","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"penis","concept":"penis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":15,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Childish euphemism for penis, derived from 'siusiać' (to urinate or wee); the universal child-directed term for the penis in Polish.","english_term":"wee-wee","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The standard term used when talking to or about young children; completely non-taboo in that register; sometimes used humorously or affectionately by adults; formed with diminutive suffix '-iak' on the root 'siu-'."}
