{"term":"wzgórek łonowy","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"mons_pubis","concept":"mons_pubis","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":95,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":58,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Polish term for the mons pubis; the rounded fatty mound of tissue over the pubic symphysis, covered by pubic hair in adults.","english_term":"mons pubis","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'pubic mound/hillock'; the adjective 'łonowy' derives from 'łono' (womb/pubic region); rarely used outside medical and anatomical education; 'łono' alone is occasionally used as a literary or archaic euphemism for the female genital area."}
{"term":"przedsionek pochwy","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vulvar_vestibule","concept":"vulvar_vestibule","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":5,"technical":98,"recognition_pct_current":28,"recognition_pct_peak":28,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Polish term for the vaginal vestibule; the space between the labia minora containing the urethral meatus and vaginal introitus.","english_term":"vaginal vestibule","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'anteroom/vestibule of the vagina'; a highly specialized anatomical term recognized almost exclusively by healthcare professionals and anatomy students; rarely encountered outside clinical or academic gynaecology."}
{"term":"błona dziewicza","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"hymen","concept":"hymen","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":10,"technical":85,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The clinical Polish term for the hymen; the thin membranous tissue partially occluding the vaginal opening.","english_term":"hymen","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'virginal membrane'; unusually high recognition for a clinical anatomical term due to the cultural and religious significance of female virginity in Polish Catholic society; frequently discussed in legal, medical, and cultural contexts."}
{"term":"narząd rodny żeński","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":2,"technical":80,"recognition_pct_current":68,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Formal clinical phrase meaning 'female reproductive organ'; encompasses the entire female reproductive and genital anatomy.","english_term":"female reproductive organ","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"A phrase rather than a single lexical item; used in formal medical, educational, and administrative writing; broader in scope than 'pochwa' (vagina alone); also serves as a formal euphemism to avoid naming specific anatomy in conservative or bureaucratic registers."}
{"term":"pizda","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":98,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The most vulgar Polish term for the vagina; simultaneously a harsh general expletive and anatomical slang, ranking among the three most taboo words in Polish alongside 'kurwa' and 'chuj'.","english_term":"cunt","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Near-universal recognition; Slavic cognates exist in Russian (пизда), Czech (pizda), Ukrainian, and others; used as expletive, insult, and anatomical reference; regularly censored in Polish broadcast media; the severity is a close match to English 'cunt' in formal severity."}
{"term":"cipa","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":82,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude Polish slang for the vagina; also used as an insult for a cowardly or contemptible person, closely paralleling English 'pussy' in both senses.","english_term":"pussy","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Somewhat less taboo than 'pizda' but strongly vulgar in polite contexts; the cowardice insult ('jesteś cipą' = you're a pussy) mirrors English usage precisely; common in colloquial speech, internet slang, and crude comedy."}
{"term":"cipka","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Diminutive of 'cipa'; crude slang for the vagina softened by the diminutive suffix, which can carry an affectionate, erotic, or infantilizing register depending on context.","english_term":"pussy","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The '-ka' diminutive suffix reduces harshness relative to 'cipa'; widely used in sexual contexts, erotic speech, and pornography; more common as an intimate or erotic term than as a standalone insult; functional overlap with English 'pussy' in affectionate sexual speech."}
{"term":"dziura","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":72,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":92,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang for the vagina, literally 'hole'; an objectifying shape metaphor emphasizing the vaginal opening as a penetrable orifice.","english_term":"hole","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Primary meaning is any physical hole or cavity; sexual use is dehumanizing and objectifying, reducing the vagina purely to its orifice function; also used colloquially for the anus in crude speech; the English 'hole' is a functionally exact parallel."}
{"term":"szpara","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":65,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang for the vagina, literally 'slit' or 'crack'; a shape metaphor emphasizing the labial cleft.","english_term":"slit","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Primary meaning is any narrow crack, crevice, or gap; the vulvar metaphor is transparent; tone and register closely parallel English 'slit'; somewhat less aggressive than 'pizda' or 'cipa' but still taboo in polite speech."}
{"term":"figa","lang":"pl","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"organs","subcategory":"vagina","concept":"vagina","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":40,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Slang for the vagina, literally 'fig'; a shape-based euphemism exploiting the visual resemblance between a split fig and the vulva.","english_term":"fig (as vulva metaphor)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The fig-as-vulva metaphor is ancient and widespread across Mediterranean and European cultures; in standard Polish 'figa' also commonly means 'nothing/zilch' ('figa z makiem' = nothing at all); the vulva sense is secondary but broadly understood; considerably milder than 'cipa' or 'pizda'."}
